There are no checks or guarantees in `getMap()` itself that prevent `this.map` from being null. Whether it is null depends entirely on how the `Environment` instance was created and used.

From the provided code, `Environment` can be constructed in a way that leaves `map` null:

- `Environment` has a constructor taking an `IMap`:
  ```java
  public Environment(final IMap map) {
    this();
    this.map = map;
    if (this.getMap() != null) {
      Game.physics().setBounds(this.getMap().getBounds());
      this.setGravity(this.getMap().getIntValue(MapProperty.GRAVITY));
    }
  }
  ```
  Here `map` is assigned directly to `this.map`. If this constructor is ever called with `map == null`, then `this.map` will be null, and `getMap()` will return null.

- The code that creates environments from `GameWorld` ensures non-null maps only in those specific call paths:
  ```java
  public Environment getEnvironment(IMap map) {
    if (map == null || map.getName() == null || map.getName().isEmpty()) {
